IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Singapore child support — the shared ontology`

-- The nouns shared by the two regimes encoded in this subject: the money
-- (sg-csp.l4) and the leave (sg-childcare-leave.l4). They are two views of
-- ONE family and they share their nouns: the same human being is the child
-- whose birth date decides a Baby Gift cohort, and the "qualifying child"
-- whose age decides a parent's childcare leave. Encoding them separately
-- would duplicate the family and let the copies disagree.

§§ `The child`

-- `birth order` is the child's order among the LIVING children of the same
-- mother, which is how the Baby Bonus Scheme counts it. It is a fact
-- supplied to the encoding, not derived: the encoding cannot see a
-- pregnancy history. See fork register F4.
DECLARE Child
    HAS `name`                        IS A STRING
        `date of birth`               IS A DATE
        `is a Singapore citizen`      IS A BOOLEAN
        `birth order`                 IS A NUMBER
        `parents lawfully married`    IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `The parent, as an employee`

-- CDCSA s 12B entitles an EMPLOYEE, not a parent in the abstract: the
-- entitlement is computed per employer per relevant period, so months
-- served is a field of the employment and not of the person.
DECLARE Parent
    HAS `name`                        IS A STRING
        `is the natural father`       IS A BOOLEAN
        `months served this period`   IS A NUMBER
        `gross daily rate`            IS A NUMBER
        `children`                    IS A LIST OF Child

§§ `Ages and years, computed the way the schemes count them`

-- Both schemes band by CALENDAR YEAR, not by exact age: the LifeSG cohort
-- table reads "children turning 1 to 17 in 2026", and Child Credits are
-- described as running "from the year the child turns 1 to the year the
-- child turns 16". So the year a child turns n is the birth year plus n,
-- and that is the unit both modules count in.
GIVEN child IS A Child, n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the year` child `turns` n MEANS
    DATE_YEAR (child's `date of birth`) PLUS n

GIVEN child IS A Child, year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the age` child `attains in` year MEANS
    year MINUS DATE_YEAR (child's `date of birth`)

-- Exact age on a day, for the leave module: CDCSA s 12B(1)(b) says "below
-- 7 years of age", which is an age on a day and not an age in a year.
GIVEN child IS A Child, day IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the age` child `has on` day MEANS
    LET  years MEANS DATE_YEAR day MINUS DATE_YEAR (child's `date of birth`)
    IN   IF   `has had a birthday by` child day
         THEN years
         ELSE years MINUS 1
    WHERE
        GIVEN c IS A Child, d IS A DATE
        GIVETH A BOOLEAN
        DECIDE `has had a birthday by` c d IF
               DATE_MONTH d GREATER THAN DATE_MONTH (c's `date of birth`)
            OR (    DATE_MONTH d EQUALS DATE_MONTH (c's `date of birth`)
                AND DATE_DAY   d AT LEAST DATE_DAY (c's `date of birth`))

§§ `The rule-version axis`

-- Every dollar figure in this subject is dated, because the subject IS a
-- transition: the whole point of the encoding is to answer "what does this
-- child get" under the rules as they stand AND under the rules as
-- announced. A reader who cannot pin the rule date cannot tell which of
-- those two answers they are being given.
GIVEN amendment IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rules in force include` amendment MEANS
    `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST amendment

§§ `Materialised interpretive forks`

-- R4 (single-instruction demo, R4-FORK-REPRESENTATION.md): where two
-- readings of the source both survive, the fork is ONE FIELD of this
-- record, threaded as an ordinary GIVEN, so both readings execute and the
-- case suite shows what turns on the difference. The fork register maps
-- these fields 1:1; two entries may not claim the same field.
-- `TYPICALLY` carries the live reading as the field's own default. That is
-- the slot a settling authority gets cited at: the day MSF publishes
-- implementation detail that decides F1, the answer goes HERE, beside the
-- field, and not into a constant somewhere else that a reader has to find.
DECLARE Interpretation
    HAS `the harmonised co-matching cap counts a lifetime total` IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY TRUE
        `the service-duration graduation survives the merger`    IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY TRUE

-- The readings this encoding takes when nobody says otherwise. It restates
-- the TYPICALLY defaults so that call sites and #ASSERTs have a value to
-- pass; the defaults above remain the normative statement of the readings.
GIVETH AN Interpretation
`the live reading` MEANS
    Interpretation WITH
        `the harmonised co-matching cap counts a lifetime total` IS TRUE
        `the service-duration graduation survives the merger`    IS TRUE
